# Pipeline Design Documentation
## Digital Mental Health NLP Pipeline for Asian Adolescents

**Author:** Lin Fang Yu 林芳伃 | MPH Candidate, NUS  
**Last Updated:** May 2026  
**Version:** 1.0

---

## Overview

This document describes the full technical and clinical design of the 3-tier Digital Mental Health NLP Pipeline for Asian Adolescents. Each design decision is grounded in clinical evidence, technical literature, and Singapore's national digital health infrastructure requirements.

The pipeline is designed to:
- Detect early mental health distress signals in Mandarin and English digital text
- Generate interpretable, clinician-reviewable risk scores
- Predict 7-day risk trajectories for proactive intervention timing
- Route identified individuals to appropriate Singapore mental health services

---

## Design Principles

| Principle | Description |
|---|---|
| **Clinical safety first** | No automated action without human review at high-risk threshold |
| **Interpretability** | Every output includes explainable feature weights |
| **Cultural validity** | Models trained and validated on Asian adolescent populations |
| **Multilingual parity** | Mandarin and English treated with equal rigour |
| **Minimal footprint** | Lightweight models deployable in resource-constrained school settings |
| **Privacy by design** | No persistent storage of raw text; anonymised feature vectors only |
| **Ecosystem integration** | Outputs map directly to existing Singapore service pathways |

